answers.workaround:
When working with tiles at the same Y coordinate, TileMaps currently break ties by rendering the oldest tiles first. With this limitation, the only way I can think of to work around this limitation is to erase all objects in the TileMap and reinsert them from left to right, something like this.

```
func xsort() -> void:
    var new_used_cells = get_used_cells().duplicate()
    new_used_cells.sort_custom(self, "_compare_by_x")

    var prev_used_cell_x: int
    if new_used_cells:
        prev_used_cell_x = new_used_cells[0].x
    for used_cell in new_used_cells:
        var tile: int = get_cell(used_cell.x, used_cell.y)
        var flip_x: bool = is_cell_x_flipped(used_cell.x, used_cell.y)
        var flip_y: bool = is_cell_y_flipped(used_cell.x, used_cell.y)
        var transpose: bool = is_cell_transposed(used_cell.x, used_cell.y)
        var autotile_coord: Vector2 = get_cell_autotile_coord(used_cell.x, used_cell.y)

        set_cell(used_cell.x, used_cell.y, -1)
        if used_cell.x != prev_used_cell_x:
            prev_used_cell_x = used_cell.x
            yield(get_tree(), "idle_frame")
        set_cell(used_cell.x, used_cell.y, tile, flip_x, flip_y, transpose, autotile_coord)
```

This code must be executed every time the scene is initialized and every time the TileMap changes. It takes a little while to run and results in a strange visible effect during its duration. It is possible there is a more efficient workaround that I have not thought of.
